12
00:42:32,727 --> 00:42:38,727
Fritz the Cat is a comic strip created by Robert Crumb.

13
00:46:05,454 --> 00:46:11,454
Set in a "supercity" of anthropomorphic animals, it focused on Fritz, a tabby cat who
frequently went on wild adventures that sometimes involved sexual escapades.

14
00:49:38,181 --> 00:49:44,181
Crumb began drawing the character in homemade comic books as a child,
and Fritz would become one of his best-known characters.

15
00:53:10,909 --> 00:53:16,909
The strip first appeared in Help!

16
00:56:43,636 --> 00:56:49,636
and Cavalier magazines, and subsequently in publications associated with the underground comix scene between 1965 and
1972; Fritz the Cat comic compilations elevated it to one of the underground scene's most iconic features.

17
01:00:16,363 --> 01:00:22,363
Fritz the Cat received further attention when it was adapted
into a 1972 animated film of the same name.

18
01:03:49,090 --> 01:03:55,090
The directorial debut of animator Ralph Bakshi, it was the first animated feature film to receive an X
rating in the United States, and the most successful independent animated feature to date.

19
01:07:21,818 --> 01:07:27,818
Crumb ended the strip later that year due to disagreements with the filmmakers.

20
01:10:54,545 --> 01:11:00,545
The film explores themes of new york city, students'
movement, underground, cat, orgy.

21
01:14:27,272 --> 01:14:33,272
The tone is lighthearted and emotional.
